From cc1db2268f9e689b64441880bbf96fb87a12cbdd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Bruno=20Greg=C3=B3rio?= <accounts@bruno.pw>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 19:01:58 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 14/16] ipa: simple: agc: Lengthen frames and add digital gain
 in low light
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The AGC stops at the longest exposure the default frame length allows
and the sensor's highest analogue gain. On an SK Hynix Hi-846 that is
33 ms and 16x, and indoor scenes come out dark, where the vendor's
camera stack goes to 66.7 ms and 128x total gain.

Add two optional tuning parameters. exposureTimeMax lets the exposure
grow past the default frame length, lengthening the frame through
VBLANK, once analogue gain is at its maximum. digitalGainMax lets the
software ISP multiply the colour gains after that. The exposure margin
the sensor keeps below the frame length is taken from the limits it
reports for the configured mode. Without the parameters nothing changes.

The software ISP applies the gains after subtracting the black level,
so black stays black. The statistics are taken from the raw frame, so
the AGC stretches their histogram by the digital gain it applies.

So that the AGC can multiply the colour gains, the IPA resets them for
every frame and Awb multiplies them rather than replacing them.

Report DigitalGain, and FrameDuration when the AGC sets the frame
length.

Signed-off-by: Bruno Gregório <accounts@bruno.pw>
